1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,399
Dinosaur bones have been

2
00:00:01,429 --> 00:00:02,888
eroding out of the ground

3
00:00:02,918 --> 00:00:04,377
for our entire existence.

4
00:00:04,757 --> 00:00:06,697
So why didn't the ancient world

5
00:00:06,727 --> 00:00:07,778
write about them?

6
00:00:08,258 --> 00:00:09,766
Egypt recorded its kings.

7
00:00:09,946 --> 00:00:11,070
Greece, its gods.

8
00:00:11,250 --> 00:00:12,374
China, its comets.

9
00:00:12,854 --> 00:00:14,042
Yet no ancient text

10
00:00:14,072 --> 00:00:15,901
clearly describes a dinosaur.

11
00:00:16,331 --> 00:00:17,470
It's not that nobody

12
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:18,523
found giant bones.

13
00:00:18,753 --> 00:00:19,402
At Mycenae,

14
00:00:19,432 --> 00:00:20,698
over 3,000 years ago,

15
00:00:20,728 --> 00:00:21,439
someone kept

16
00:00:21,469 --> 00:00:23,167
a fossil rhino's ankle bone.

17
00:00:23,447 --> 00:00:25,161
Egyptians wrapped fossil bones

18
00:00:25,191 --> 00:00:25,684
in linen,

19
00:00:25,714 --> 00:00:26,672
perhaps as relics

20
00:00:26,702 --> 00:00:27,544
of the god Set.

21
00:00:27,824 --> 00:00:28,864
Emperor Augustus collected

22
00:00:28,894 --> 00:00:30,017
so-called "bones of giants."

23
00:00:30,247 --> 00:00:30,651
In China,

24
00:00:30,681 --> 00:00:31,666
fossil "dragon bones"

25
00:00:31,696 --> 00:00:32,439
became medicine.

26
00:00:32,869 --> 00:00:34,094
But most were mammals,

27
00:00:34,124 --> 00:00:35,521
like mammoths and rhinos:

28
00:00:35,551 --> 00:00:36,206
far younger,

29
00:00:36,236 --> 00:00:37,918
and far closer to the surface.

30
00:00:38,248 --> 00:00:39,422
Fossilization is rare.

31
00:00:39,452 --> 00:00:40,681
After 66 million years,

32
00:00:40,711 --> 00:00:41,721
most dinosaur bones

33
00:00:41,751 --> 00:00:42,652
lie deep in rock,

34
00:00:42,682 --> 00:00:43,582
and what surfaces

35
00:00:43,612 --> 00:00:44,567
is usually broken.

36
00:00:45,297 --> 00:00:46,421
The real answer?

37
00:00:47,151 --> 00:00:48,007
Finding a fossil

38
00:00:48,037 --> 00:00:49,113
and understanding it

39
00:00:49,143 --> 00:00:50,109
are two completely

40
00:00:50,139 --> 00:00:51,050
different things.

41
00:00:51,580 --> 00:00:52,787
Recognizing a dinosaur

42
00:00:52,817 --> 00:00:53,855
takes ideas science

43
00:00:53,885 --> 00:00:55,316
only accepted around 1800:

44
00:00:55,346 --> 00:00:55,935
extinction,

45
00:00:55,965 --> 00:00:57,621
and an unimaginably old Earth.

46
00:00:58,051 --> 00:00:58,988
Without them,

47
00:00:59,018 --> 00:01:01,146
a giant bone becomes a giant.

48
00:01:01,176 --> 00:01:02,039
Or a dragon.

49
00:01:02,069 --> 00:01:03,378
Maybe even a myth.

50
00:01:03,908 --> 00:01:04,751
Even in 1677,

51
00:01:04,781 --> 00:01:06,497
an Oxford scholar examined

52
00:01:06,527 --> 00:01:08,445
a broken dinosaur thigh bone,

53
00:01:08,475 --> 00:01:09,721
and decided it came

54
00:01:09,751 --> 00:01:10,997
from a giant human.

55
00:01:11,277 --> 00:01:12,526
The word "dinosaur"

56
00:01:12,556 --> 00:01:14,210
wasn't coined until 1842.

57
00:01:15,040 --> 00:01:16,484
So maybe ancient people

58
00:01:16,514 --> 00:01:17,702
did find dinosaurs.

59
00:01:18,132 --> 00:01:19,641
They just couldn't know

60
00:01:19,671 --> 00:01:21,179
what they were holding.

61
00:01:22,009 --> 00:01:23,558
How many discoveries came

62
00:01:23,588 --> 00:01:25,579
before we could understand them?

63
00:01:26,209 --> 00:01:27,376
And which ones

64
00:01:27,406 --> 00:01:29,513
are we holding right now?
